sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: B3 (migration-dashboard/public/task-vms.html, "What is baked in" and the decision log), docs/reference/compute-drivers.md (The topology seed), swamp/proof/reset/README.md.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten rules 1, 3, 6, 9, 20, 22 (the host work is one placed step per action group; the build is the proof reset of the release, not a second builder; the swap is renames in one step; one end check).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · refresh the topology seed of a task VM host
  %% kind: task_vm.seed_refresh
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane B icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>over ssh
  participant B as Builder VM<br/>task image
  Note over GW: Validate, record the run
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - the release flow, for every task VM host in parallel, once the release is live and the host's task image is promoted (B8)
  %%> - there is no CLI command of its own; an operator runs `php artisan task-vms:refresh-seed HOST` on the Gateway
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the host is an active task VM host with a promoted task image, and no image build is running on it
  %%> - the host has the seed of that image: a refresh starts from it and never from nothing
  %%> - the host has the three slots of a large VM free, and no image build holds the host lock
  %%> - the Gateway runs a release commit, the GitHub App can read the release repository, and the proof Swamp Club key is set
  %%> Result:
  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent; a second admission for the host returns the open task
  %%> - nothing on the host changes yet
  GW->>SW: Push the credentials<br/>vault: GitHub header, club key
  %%> Why:
  %%> - credentials go Gateway → vault → step; the run inputs hold only vault key names
  %%> - the GitHub read header is minted by the Gateway's App, whose key never leaves the Gateway
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-seed-refresh
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the task image, the names of the current, building and previous seed, and the builder VM with its instance config
  %%> - the release repository and the commit the Gateway runs, so the ready topology is the release it will serve
  SW->>H: Remove a stale builder<br/>ssh exec · cleanup-builder.sh
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - takes the host seed lock, the lock a claim's seed clone takes
  %%> - deletes the builder VM
  %%> - repairs a promotion that died between its two renames: if the current seed is missing, the old seed goes back from `-previous`
  %%> - only then deletes an unpromoted `-refresh` clone
  %%> - keeps `-previous`, the rollback copy
  %%> Why:
  %%> - a host refreshes one seed at a time, so anything with these names belongs to an earlier run that ended badly
  %%> The script:
  %%> - the file `tools/task-vm-seed/cleanup-builder.sh` travels in the intent, so the run shows what it ran
  SW->>H: Clone the seed<br/>ssh exec · volume copy
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - takes the host seed lock, then copies the current seed to the building name; on ZFS the copy is a clone and costs only what the build writes
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the build starts from the node template that is in the seed, and the old seed stays untouched
  %%> Fails when:
  %%> - the host has no current seed; nothing has changed
  SW->>H: Start the builder<br/>ssh exec · incus init, start
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - creates a large VM (12 GiB, 6 CPUs) from the task image, with the seed clone as the disk `topology-seed`, and starts it
  %%> - waits until the Incus agent reports the VM's address
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - no fleet identity: the builder has no WireGuard address, Node, token or model key, and it is not an enrolled slot
  SW->>B: Fetch the source<br/>incus exec · git clone
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - clones the release repository into the checkout path and checks out the release commit on the branch `seed-refresh/task-1`
  %%> Why the branch:
  %%> - the topology harness refuses a checkout whose branch does not name its task
  %%> Secret:
  %%> - the GitHub read header reaches Git from standard input; it is in no argument and no file
  SW->>B: Prepare the builder<br/>incus exec · prepare-builder.sh
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - installs the proof Swamp Club key for the reset's Swamp runs, from standard input, mode 0600
  %%> - adds idle stand-ins for the observer and Pi services and a throwaway `orbit` interface
  %%> Why the stand-ins:
  %%> - the reset proves it leaves the outer VM's services and tunnel as it found them, and a builder has none of them
  SW->>B: Install the dependencies<br/>incus exec · composer install
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - installs the Composer packages of the SDK, the CLI, the harness and the Gateway, in that order
  SW->>B: Build the topology<br/>incus exec · bin/proof-fleet
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - adopts the seed clone as the Incus pool `topology-seed`
  %%> - runs the proof reset of the release: Gateway with Swamp, app-dev and app-prod, real WireGuard on 10.45.0.0/24
  %%> - the reset ends when the fleet is healthy, its agents are online and Doctor is clean
  %%> Measured:
  %%> - 6 min 33 s to 7 min 26 s on beast at host load 5 to 9, in whole runs of 7 min 31 s and 10 min 2 s; the S3 measurement of the reset alone is 232 s on a quiet host
  SW->>B: Make the ready copies<br/>incus exec · ready-copies.sh
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - stops the three VMs and keeps a stopped copy of each as `ready-gateway`, `ready-app-dev` and `ready-app-prod`
  %%> - deletes the three VMs of the build and the operator VM the harness still makes; the node template stays
  %%> - prints the list of the ready copies
  %%> The copy interface (for the reset from ready copies):
  %%> - no NIC: a static MAC or address cannot sit on a second instance, and a NIC on the build's network stops a new task VM from adopting the pool; the values are in `user.orbit.ready.mac`, `user.orbit.ready.ipv4` and `user.orbit.ready.network`, and the role in `user.orbit.ready.role`
  %%> - the disk `orbit-source` points at the fixed path `/home/orbit/.orbit/ready/source`
  %%> - the build's source tree, with its `.env`, `storage`, `bootstrap/cache`, ACLs and owners, is kept in the pool as the custom volume `ready-source`; the ready Gateway needs it, because its baked database is encrypted with the application key in that `.env`, and a task VM restores it to the fixed path before it starts the copies
  %%> - no `user.orbit.e2e.*` label, so a release of a build never deletes a ready copy
  SW->>B: Check the ready copies<br/>incus exec · check-ready.sh
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts a throwaway copy of each ready copy and waits for its Incus agent, so each ready copy is a VM that boots
  %%> - searches each copy, and the volume `ready-source`, for the Swamp Club key of the build, by path and by value
  %%> - deletes the copies; a ready copy itself is never started
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the seed is cloned into every task VM of the host, so a key in it would reach all of them
  %%> - a ready copy that does not boot must not become the seed
  %%> Prints:
  %%> - the roles that passed, which the Gateway compares with the roles it admitted
  SW->>H: Stop the builder<br/>ssh exec · incus stop
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - stops the builder cleanly; its Incus unmounts the pool, so the seed holds a finished file system
  SW->>H: Promote the seed<br/>ssh exec · promote-seed.sh
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - takes the host seed lock, so no claim clones the seed between the renames
  %%> - deletes the builder; its seed clone stays as a volume
  %%> - `zfs promote` makes the clone own the blocks it shares with the old seed, so a chain of refreshes does not grow
  %%> - deletes the `-previous` seed of the refresh before, then renames the current seed to `-previous` and the clone to the seed name
  %%> - ignores HUP, INT and TERM during the two renames; if the second rename fails, the first is undone
  %%> - prints the seed name, its size and the bytes the build wrote
  %%> Rollback copy:
  %%> - the old seed stays as `-previous` until the next successful refresh deletes it
  %%> - a task VM that runs keeps its own clone of the old seed, even after that deletion
  %%> The only change:
  %%> - this is the only step that changes what a claim clones
  SW->>H: Remove the builder<br/>ssh exec · cleanup-builder.sh
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - runs after success, failure and cancellation, with the same script as the first step
  %%> - repairs a promotion that died between its renames before it deletes anything
  %%> - deletes the builder VM and an unpromoted clone; a promoted clone has the seed name by now
  %%> - lists the VMs and volumes for the end check
  Note over SW: Check the seed<br/>assert
  %%> End check:
  %%> - a native `assert` over the step results
  %%> - the build, the ready copies, their check, the promotion and the cleanup passed
  %%> - the host lists the seed, and no builder and no unpromoted clone
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the task succeeds, with the seed name, its rollback copy, the ready copies, the size of the seed and the bytes the build wrote
    %%> - the Gateway keeps no other state: the seed is on the host, under the name every claim clones
    %%> The next claim:
    %%> - clones the new seed and boots a VM that holds the three ready VMs
  else a step fails
    H--xSW: exit ≠ 0
    %%> When:
    %%> - a host command, the build, the ready copies, the promotion or the end check fails, or the run is cancelled
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    Note over GW: Record failure
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the task fails; no alert fires
    %%> On the host:
    %%> - the host keeps the seed it had, because the promotion is the last host change and undoes itself
    %%> - the builder and its clone are deleted by the cleanup, and the next run removes any a crash left
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the refresh again after fixing the cause; it starts from the current seed
  end
